diff --git a/CONTRIBUTING.md b/CONTRIBUTING.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..898a0d0a00689e5d31cfd92609778ecdacd96d21
--- /dev/null
+++ b/CONTRIBUTING.md
@@ -0,0 +1,44 @@
+# Contributing to Hornhoof Edge
+
+Small fixes and clear bug reports are welcome. For a large change, open an issue first so we can agree on what it should do. Security problems go through [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md).
+
+## How a change gets in
+
+1. Fork [the repository](https://github.com/Invariant0x/hornhoof-edge) and make a branch from `main` named after the change.
+2. Write a failing test first, run it and see it fail, then write the code that makes it pass.
+3. Run the checks below.
+4. Sign off every commit with `git commit -s`.
+5. Open a pull request against `main` and fill in the template. Keep it to one topic.
+6. A maintainer reviews the change. Answer review comments with new commits, and do not force-push over a branch under review.
+7. A maintainer merges it as one commit with the pull request's title. Your sign-off goes with it.
+
+## Checks before you push
+
+```sh
+sh relay/test/frp.sh
+python3 -m unittest discover -s relay/test -v
+shellcheck relay/setup-relay.sh relay/hornhoof-relay relay/test/frp.sh
+```
+
+Use Python 3.12 or newer. The tests run local servers and need no Python packages. The first command downloads the pinned frp binaries and checks their SHA-256.
+
+## Rules for code and words
+
+- Code, comments and documentation are in English, with no em dashes.
+- Use only Python's standard library, and keep the relay runnable on Python 3.12.
+- Reformat only files you change.
+- Put no private value in a change: no server address, private hostname, company link name, key, token, password, certificate or access configuration. Examples use hornhoof.org and documentation addresses.
+- `relay/test/test_public.py` checks every file Git would publish. Private values listed in a local `EDGE_PRIVATE_WORDS` file or `~/.hornhoof-edge/private-words` are checked too, without printing them.
+- If a change adds to or changes what the relay stores, update the README's list in the same pull request.
+
+## Sign your work
+
+Every commit carries a `Signed-off-by` line. It says that you wrote the change, or have the right to send it, under the [Developer Certificate of Origin](https://developercertificate.org/). Git adds it using the name and email in your settings:
+
+```sh
+git commit -s -m "Explain what the change does"
+```
+
+The commit message is a plain sentence in English. Leave generated-by and co-author lines out. You answer for every line you send, including work written with an AI agent.
+
+By sending a pull request, you agree that your contribution is published under the [MIT License](LICENSE). Everyone here follows the [code of conduct](CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md).
diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..ce17361a39bb2cb6336a5188ff9a14cdecf5f25d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,63 @@
+# Hornhoof Edge
+
+The relay behind the public links of [Hornhoof](https://github.com/Invariant0x/hornhoof) companies.
+
+## What it does
+
+It gives each company a link like `https://acme.hornhoof.org`. The company's own machine opens a tunnel to the relay, so nothing needs to open on the company's router. A visitor opens the link, and the relay passes the request through that company's tunnel to its Hornhoof app.
+
+The relay hands out names, checks each tunnel's key, and sends each request to the company named in its address.
+
+## What it sees
+
+At present, the browser's https connection ends at hornhoof.org. The relay reads each request to find its company, then passes it through that company's tunnel. This includes the contents of requests and answers. It keeps no copy of what passes.
+
+This is the same behavior described in [Hornhoof's security page](https://github.com/Invariant0x/hornhoof/blob/main/SECURITY.md#the-hornhooforg-link).
+
+## What it never does
+
+- Run a company's code.
+- Hold a company's data, files or passwords in storage.
+- Keep accounts.
+- Reach through a tunnel to anything but the Hornhoof app.
+- Keep a copy of the requests and answers it passes.
+
+## What it stores
+
+For each link, the relay keeps:
+
+- its name and a hash of its key;
+- when and from which address it was made;
+- when its tunnel was last seen;
+- whether it is blocked, when it was blocked, and why.
+
+For each address, it also keeps the times of new links made in the last hour, to limit how many it can make. The relay's database is SQLite.
+
+The relay logs each link made or freed with the address it came from. frps logs each tunnel's login. On the server, both logs go to the system journal.
+
+## Run the tests
+
+You need Python 3.12 or newer, Git, curl and tar, on macOS or Linux. The tests use only Python's standard library. The first run downloads frps and frpc 0.71.1 from their release and checks the archive's SHA-256 before using it.
+
+From this folder:
+
+```sh
+sh relay/test/frp.sh
+python3 -m unittest discover -s relay/test -v
+```
+
+The tests start their own relay, frps, frpc and small web server on local ports, then stop them. They never connect to the public relay. The publication checks read tracked files and files Git would add, respecting `.gitignore`. If `EDGE_PRIVATE_WORDS` names a file, or `~/.hornhoof-edge/private-words` exists, they also check that none of its private values appears in those files.
+
+## What is where
+
+- `relay/relay.py`: the name API, tunnel checks and operator commands, in one Python file.
+- `relay/frps.toml`: frps settings; the server password is a placeholder.
+- `relay/setup-relay.sh`, `relay/hornhoof-relay` and the service files: server setup and operator commands.
+- `Caddyfile`: the web server settings, the API and tunnel entry points, and company traffic.
+- `pages/`: the page template, build script and fonts. `pages/build.py` builds `www/index.html` and the relay's 404 and 410 pages.
+- `relay/test/`: tests and their local server harness. Downloaded binaries stay in the ignored `.bin/` folder.
+- `deploy.env.example`: example deployment settings. Real settings, access keys and other private files stay outside this repository.
+
+## License
+
+The code is under the [MIT License](LICENSE). The Onest and Tektur fonts are under [OFL-1.1](pages/fonts/OFL.txt).
diff --git a/SECURITY.md b/SECURITY.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..fecdec9b4c35ad23358dc617b75483ac07581f2c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/SECURITY.md
@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+# Security
+
+Hornhoof Edge carries the public links of Hornhoof companies. What it sees and what it stores are listed in the [README](README.md#what-it-stores).
+
+## In scope
+
+- `relay/relay.py`, including the name API and frps plugin.
+- The frps and Caddy settings.
+- The setup script and operator commands.
+- The pages the relay serves.
+
+## Report a vulnerability
+
+Report it privately on [the repository](https://github.com/Invariant0x/hornhoof-edge), rather than in a public issue: open the **Security** tab and click **Report a vulnerability**. Only the maintainers see the report.
+
+Tell us:
+
+- what an attacker can do, and what they need first, such as a link or its key;
+- the steps to show it, as short as you can make them;
+- the commit you run and the frp version;
+- whether it touches the relay API, tunnel checks, frps, Caddy, setup or the pages.
+
+We aim to answer within a week, agree on a fix and a date with you, and name you in the fix unless you ask us not to.
+
+You are welcome to test this on your own local relay.
+
+## Which versions
+
+Only the latest code on `main` gets security fixes.
